      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to count the number of ways to distribute 6 identical books between the library and checked out, with at least one book in the library and at least one in checked out.\n\nFirst, we note that since there are 6 books and at least one must be in the library and at least one must be checked out, the distribution must be 1 book to the library and 5 books to be checked out, or vice versa. This is one way to distribute the books.\n\nThus, there are exactly 2 ways: either all 6 books are in the library or all 6 books are checked out. Since each scenario involves the same indistinguishable books, any other distribution of 1 and 5 among 6 books would count as a duplicate of one of these two.\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to distribute these books is \\boxed{2}.",
