    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let the number of books in the library be . Since books checked out, there are books checked out.\n\nTherefore, there are \\begin{align*}\n\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }{360 {}_{6}C_{1}60{}_{5}C_{3}}}\n\\end{align*} different ways for some of the books to be checked out and the rest to be checked in.\n\nTo solve this problem, we can use the concept of combinations. We are given six identical copies of a certain book and need to determine the number of ways to distribute them between being in the library and being checked out.\n\nLet's denote the number of books in the library as . Since books checked out, there are books checked out.\n\nWe need to find the number of ways to choose books to be in the library and the rest to be checked out, i.e., the number of ways to distribute  books among six.\n\nWe can use the combination formula to calculate this: \n\\begin{align*}\n{6 \\choose 1} \\times {5 \\choose 3} = 6 \\times {5 \\choose 3} = \\boxed{360}\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, there are 360 different ways to distribute the books between being in the library and being checked out.",
